proxy_service: an image the store does not hold is a 404 at once, offline or store-only

A game's icon or an achievement's badge is asked of this process on the
path RetroArch uses (/Images/..., /Badge/..., /UserPic/...) and answered
from the image cache when it is there. When it was not, the process asked
is_online(), and if the answer was yes fetched the image from
media.retroachievements.org with a ten-second timeout -- ten seconds that
do not bound the name lookup -- before answering 204. is_online() is not
free either: while the reachability tracker says unreachable it probes on
every call (network.should_probe_retroachievements), a HEAD to
retroachievements.org with a five-second timeout of the same kind, spared
only when no interface but lo has operstate "up"; and the request handler
had already asked it once more, for its log line. So on a link that is up
and leads nowhere -- a hotspot with no data, a router whose uplink is down,
the fifteen seconds after any drop before the monitor's next probe -- an
image the store does not hold costs a fetch that has to fail, or two
probes: tens of seconds, and ROCKNIX's summary page asks for one per
visible row while it shows the device's copy (fork #199).

Now such a miss is answered 404 at once, with no probe and no upstream
attempt, when the request carries X-RA-Store-Only: 1 (patch 010's header,
which ROCKNIX's interface sends on every request whose host is this proxy)
or when this process's own state is offline -- the tracker as the monitor
last left it, the same state online_state.json carries -- and the handler's
log line reads that state instead of probing for an image request. A miss
while the state is online fetches and caches as before; a hit is served as
before; a dorequest.php call is untouched; nothing else changes. 404 rather
than the 204 a failed fetch gives: RetroArch writes a badge for a 200 only
(cheevos_client.c) and logs both alike, and a client that saves the body to
a file keeps a non-2xx answer out of its image cache, where a 204 lands as
an empty file it then has to notice and remove (ROCKNIX #180).

No lock serialises these requests: the runtime server is a
socketserver.ThreadingMixIn TCPServer, one thread per connection, and the
tracker's lock is held for a read. The 137 s that opened #199 -- four
/Images/ requests logged in one second, nothing for 137 s, the list "134 s
after A" -- was not this process waiting: the proof's own frames show the
list on screen 1.7 s after A (the frame it took for the wait card is
byte-identical to the list frame it took 135 s later), so its timer was
waiting for a change from a screen that had already changed, and the key
press that ended the wait scrolled new rows, and new icon requests, into
view. The link there was down, so the probe short-circuited on operstate
and each miss was a 204 at once; the cost this patch removes is the one a
link that is up and dead would have charged.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue maxengel/rocknix#199). Offered upstream
as-is (ROCKNIX D-RA-002, D-RA-016: whatever the fork changes in the proxy
goes back).

--- a/linux/raofflineproxy/proxy_service.py
+++ b/linux/raofflineproxy/proxy_service.py
